## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to fuse graph shortest-path ideas with node activation delays and cycle handling, plus choosing between DAG DP and general graph Dijkstra.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra with transformed edge costs w(u,v)+d(v)
- Topological sort for DAG optimization
- Handling multiple sources S
- Non-negative weights guarantee correctness

## Code Example
```javascript
function earliestFinish(V, adj, S, d) {
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = new Array(V).fill(INF);
  const pq = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a[0]-b[0]);
  for (const s of S) { dist[s] = d[s]; pq.push([dist[s], s]); }
  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    const [du, u] = pq.pop();
    if (du !== dist[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      const cand = dist[u] + w + d[v];
      if (cand < dist[v]) { dist[v] = cand; pq.push([cand, v]); }
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
```

## Concept Overview
An LRU (Least Recently Used) cache efficiently manages memory by evicting the least accessed items when capacity is reached. This implementation leverages a HashMap for fast key lookups and a DoublyLinkedList for maintaining access order.

## Implementation Details
- **HashMap**: Maps keys to Node references, enabling O(1) lookup operations
- **DoublyLinkedList**: Maintains access order with head representing most recently used items and tail representing least recently used items
- **Thread Safety**: ReentrantReadWriteLock provides optimal concurrency - multiple threads can read simultaneously while writes are mutually exclusive
- **Eviction Policy**: When capacity is exceeded, the tail node (least recently used) is removed
- **Operations**: Both get() and put() operations maintain O(1) time complexity

## Code Example
```java
public class LRUCache<K, V> {
    private final Map<K, Node<K, V>> cache;
    private final DoublyLinkedList<K, V> order;
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock lock;
    private final int capacity;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.cache = new HashMap<>();
        this.order = new DoublyLinkedList<>();
        this.lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        lock.readLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> node = cache.get(key);
            if (node != null) {
                // Upgrade to write lock for reordering
                lock.readLock().unlock();
                lock.writeLock().lock();
                order.moveToHead(node);
                return node.value;
            }
            return null;
        } finally {
            if (lock.isWriteLocked()) {
                lock.writeLock().unlock();
            } else {
                lock.readLock().unlock();
            }
        }
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> existing = cache.get(key);
            if (existing != null) {
                existing.value = value;
                order.moveToHead(existing);
                return;
            }

            Node<K, V> newNode = new Node<>(key, value);
            cache.put(key, newNode);
            order.addToHead(newNode);

            if (cache.size() > capacity) {
                Node<K, V> tail = order.removeTail();
                cache.remove(tail.key);
            }
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of handling duplicate-heavy inputs efficiently and the ability to implement in-place sorting algorithms with optimized space usage.

## Key Concepts
- **3-way partitioning**: Separates elements into three groups: less than, equal to, and greater than the pivot
- **In-place quicksort**: Achieves O(1) auxiliary space (excluding recursion stack)
- **Pivot selection**: Random sampling avoids worst-case behavior on sorted inputs; median-of-three provides more robust estimates
- **Complexity analysis**: Best/average O(n log n); worst O(n²) when pivot consistently produces imbalanced partitions

## Code Example
```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random() * (hi - lo + 1)) + lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo, gt = hi;
  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) {
      [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]];
      lt++; i++;
    } else if (a[i] > pivot) {
      [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]];
      gt--;
    } else {
      i++;
    }
  }
  threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
  threeWayQuicksort(a, gt + 1, hi);
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Best case**: O(n log n) — partitions remain balanced
- **Average case**: O(n log n) — expected with random pivot
- **Worst case**: O(n²) — occurs with sorted input and poor pivot (e.g., always picking first or last element)

## Pivot Selection Trade-offs
| Strategy | Pros | Cons |
|----------|------|------|
| Random | Simple, avoids adversarial input | Still possible to pick poor pivots |
| Median-of-three | More robust estimates | Slightly more computation |

The 3-way partition is particularly valuable for arrays with many duplicates, as it reduces unnecessary swaps and comparisons for elements equal to the pivot.

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your understanding of counting DP, covering both memoization (top-down) and tabulation (bottom-up) approaches, along with space optimization and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts

- Subset sum counting with each element used at most once
- Memoization (top-down) with recursive DFS and memo table
- Tabulation (bottom-up) with 1D space optimization to O(S)
- Modulo arithmetic to prevent integer overflow

## Edge Cases

- **Empty subset (N=0)**: If N=0 and S=0, return 1 (one valid subset - empty set). If S>0, return 0.
- **Target S=0**: Always return 1 (the empty subset counts as one valid subset).
- **All elements greater than S**: No valid subsets exist, return 0.

## Code Example

```javascript
function countSubsets(nums, target) {
  const mod = 1_000_000_007;
  const n = nums.length;

  // Top-down with memoization
  const memo = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(target + 1).fill(-1));
  function dfs(i, s) {
    if (s === 0) return 1;
    if (i === 0) return 0;
    if (memo[i][s] !== -1) return memo[i][s];
    memo[i][s] = dfs(i - 1, s) % mod;
    if (s >= nums[i - 1]) {
      memo[i][s] = (memo[i][s] + dfs(i - 1, s - nums[i - 1])) % mod;
    }
    return memo[i][s];
  }
  return dfs(n, target);
}

// Bottom-up with space optimization
function countSubsetsBU(nums, target) {
  const mod = 1_000_000_007;
  const dp = Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;

  for (const num of nums) {
    for (let s = target; s >= num; s--) {
      dp[s] = (dp[s] + dp[s - num]) % mod;
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```

Both solutions run in O(N×S) time with the bottom-up approach using O(S) space.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on strings, demonstrating memoization vs tabulation and optimization tricks.

## Key Concepts
- Pattern matching with Trie to prune dictionary checks
- Top-down vs bottom-up DP on string indices
- Handling empty inputs and modulo arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWays(s, dict){
  const trie = buildTrie(dict);
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[n] = 1;
  for(let i = n-1; i >= 0; --i){
    let node = trie;
    for(let j = i; j < n; j++){
      const c = s[j];
      if(!node.next[c]) break;
      node = node.next[c];
      if(node.end) dp[i] += dp[j+1];
    }
  }
  return dp[0];
}
function buildTrie(words){
  const root = {next:{}, end:false};
  for(const w of words){
    let node = root;
    for(const ch of w){
      if(!node.next[ch]) node.next[ch] = {next:{}, end:false};
      node = node.next[ch];
    }
    node.end = true;
  }
  return root;
}
```

